      "id": 1,
      "text": "Dayton was incorporated on January 13, 1844, after early settlement began in the 1830s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dayton%2C_Alabama"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s historic Half‑Chance Bridge and William Poole House both sit on the National Register of Historic Places.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/dayton/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1886, local investors unsuccessfully tried to bring a railroad from Faunsdale to Dayton, a plan that never materialized.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/dayton/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Dayton’s town hall, originally the Boddie Law Office, is listed on Alabama’s Register of Landmarks and Heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/dayton/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town sits on a ridge that drains southeast to Dry Creek and northwest to Little Dry Creek, tributaries of Chickasaw Bogue.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dayton%2C_Alabama"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Sweet Water Creek, after which Dayton is named, flows through the town and gave the area its early settlers a vital water source.",
